    "body": "> StructuredSerializeWithTransfer(null, « new ArrayBuffer(2) »)\r\n\r\n1. We put a reference in memory for the ArrayBuffer, in case it gets referenced by the first argument passed (it doesn't, but we don't know that).\r\n2. We serialize the value. Any references to to-be-transferred values will hit the memory map so the algorithm doesn't need to concern itself with those.\r\n3. We update the memory for transferred values with proper serializations. We do this after the fact in case the previous step threw an exception. We also collect all the transferred values in an array, so we can pass the references to the caller. (We indeed need to fix this to update the memory structure.)\r\n\r\n> StructuredSerializeWithTransfer({ x: { y: someArrayBuffer } }, someArrayBuffer), i.e. a case that requires recursion.\r\n\r\n1. Put a reference in memory for someArrayBuffer, same as before.\r\n2. Structured clone value. By the time it gets to someArrayBuffer, that'll be returned from memory.\r\n3. Same as before.",
